  "generated_text": "First, we find the slope of the tangent to the parabola at $A = (1,1).$  The derivative of the parabola is $y' = 2x,$ so the slope of the tangent at $A$ is $2 \\cdot 1 = 2.$  Since the normal is perpendicular to the tangent, the slope of the normal is $-1/2.$\n\nThe equation of the normal at $A$ is $y - 1 = -1/2 (x - 1),$ which simplifies to $y = -1/2 x + 3/2.$\n\nTo find the intersection of the normal with the parabola, we set $y = x^2$ equal to $y = -1/2 x + 3/2.$  This gives $x^2 = -1/2 x + 3/2,$ which simplifies to $x^2 + x - 3 = 0.$  Factoring, we get $(x + 3)(x - 1) = 0,$ so $x = -3$ or $1.$  Therefore, $B = \\boxed{(-3/2,9/4)}.$",
